# TSan suppressions — documented third-party races (the workflow's triage
# policy: real defects are fixed in code; third-party/system-call findings
# get a documented suppression here).
#
# Created 2026-11-09 after tsan-nightly's FIRST real run (the job had been
# dispatch-only since creation — the cron was only added 2026-11-07). Both
# findings are trantor-internal (Drogon's event-loop library, Conan-pinned
# trantor, unpatched by us):

# 1. EventLoop::wakeup() writes the wakeup eventfd from arbitrary threads
#    while createEventfd() set it up on another — the fd is DESIGNED for
#    cross-thread signalling; TSan flags the write()-interceptor-level
#    non-atomicity, not an application race.
#    Evidence (run 42469207432): Read via wakeup() EventLoop.cc:365 vs
#    Previous write via createEventfd() EventLoop.cc:55, threads T1/T2.
race:trantor::EventLoop::wakeup

# 2. MpscQueue handoff move-constructs the queued std::function on the
#    producer side while the consumer may still see the node — the queue's
#    publication protocol is the synchronization; the move's internal
#    pointer write races only at the TSan interceptor level.
#    Evidence (same run): std::function move ctor std_function.h:405 vs
#    move-assign :489, both under trantor::MpscQueue BufferNode paths in
#    fulla-tests.
race:trantor::MpscQueue

# If new findings appear, triage them: first-party stacks (libs/**,
# apps/**) are FIXED, not suppressed. Extend this file only with upstream
# evidence and a dated comment like the two above.
